    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "d9513f6d-6eda-3a13-a9d0-970e0f201a96",
                "word": "Shantung",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                        "entry_id": "d9513f6d-6eda-3a13-a9d0-970e0f201a96",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of Shandong."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "694778c6-0399-490b-aa04-a96e5c60abdd",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "Wu defected on Oct. 16, when he took off on a training flight from his home base in Shantung and flew to South Korea."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a4361361-e9c3-4291-b6c9-6ec0a39c5234",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "The rest of the coast of China northward, as far as this chart is continued, is copied from the Missionary provincial charts, except in the direction of the coast, which is made more westerly, to accord with the situation of the Chantong or Shantung Promontory, at the entrance of the Yellow Sea, as observed in the voyage of Lord MACARTNEY."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9293be56-2186-4efb-b66e-e0bd3756d5f4",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "(4) Ting Pao-cheng, lieut.-governor of Shantung, reports having sacrificed to the Tai Shan (泰山), inspected the Confucian Temple at Chüfow (the native place of Confucius), which has just undergone a thorough repair, and examined the dykes erected to protect the low lands, in the neighbourhood of Yuncheng, from the inundations of the Yellow River."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "06608aa0-6a06-415c-bd3b-2feb4846199c",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "We uttered our chagrin that the spokesmen for the American conscience—aye, for the \"conscience of civilization\"—had sanctioned the confessed immorality of the Shantung award to satisfy a secret covenant against which we righteously proclaimed, and we did all we can do to right the wrong."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ae5b7cf6-59c9-4ac6-9dd7-95ca5493e34f",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "The first step looking toward the partition of such a state as China is likely to be the marking out of the country into spheres of special interest, and these several agreements between the Powers and China resulted in the application of that term to various parts of the Empire. Thus Manchuria was said to be Russia's sphere of interest, Shantung the sphere of Germany, etc."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5df8e2b8-cfd1-44f7-9554-cf9884226f0b",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "Thus far it has been impossible to trace more accurately the development during these five centuries as archeological data, which would have provided a better basis for study, have been lacking. It has only been in the last few years that field work has given us some necessary information, particularly in Shantung Province, and it may be reasonably anticipated that further studies by the scholar, Wan Hsin-t'ang, from Chi-nan, will throw much light on this chapter of Chinese art."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6abf9e2c-60c3-4159-bebe-4f97611c2db5",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "Taking advantage of the European war in 1914, Japan seized the German possessions in Shantung, which she promptly enlarged."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ed82aa7-a8a9-46eb-89cd-48b35706d9fb",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "Wagner was a German agricultural economist who in 1911 went to Tsingtao to teach in a German-Chinese Middle School. He later worked at the Litsun Agricultural Experimental Station in Shantung where he continued his studies of agriculture both past and present."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7c3664ab-ed01-4f3b-9dfa-8c23dea13ebb",
                                "sense_id": "b7680c06-6d47-335b-a717-ccde85644a22",
                                "text": "In 1877, Chefoo's exports came from the town's agricultural hinterland of the Shantung Province."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
